---
import StarlightPage from '@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro';
import Playground from '../components/demos/Playground.astro';
import Bench from '../components/demos/Bench.astro';
import Diagnostics from '../components/demos/Diagnostics.astro';
---

<StarlightPage
  frontmatter={{
    title: 'Live demos',
    description:
      'The engine, running in your browser: a book you can edit, a bench you can run, and the diagnostics channel with something wrong in it.',
    tableOfContents: false,
  }}
>
  <p>
    fleuron claims a book paginates in hundreds of milliseconds, and that
    the preview matches the export. You can check both on your own machine,
    so check them here.
  </p>

  <p>
    Everything below runs the real engine, compiled to WebAssembly, in a
    worker. The worker holds the module and never touches an element. The
    page holds the DOM and never sees the engine. What crosses between them
    is a display structure, and the SVG on screen is painted from it.
  </p>

  <h2>The playground</h2>

  <p>
    A chapter of <em>Pride and Prejudice</em> and the stylesheet that sets it.
    Change either and the book is laid out again, a fifth of a second after
    you stop typing. You can close the stylesheet editor from the settings.
    What you end up with is in the URL, so a question about the CSS subset
    can be a link.
  </p>

  <div class="demo-frame">
    <Playground demo="playground" />
  </div>

  <h2>The bench</h2>

  <p>
    <em>Pride and Prejudice</em> entire, fetched and laid out here. Each row
    says which stages the engine ran for it, taken from the engine's own
    counters, so you can tell a reused stage from one that ran. The machine
    is named, because a timing without one tells you nothing.
  </p>

  <div class="demo-frame">
    <Bench />
  </div>

  <h2>Diagnostics</h2>

  <p>
    A declaration outside <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}css-subset/`}
      >the subset</a
    > is reported at the line and column it was written at, and the book lays out
    anyway. Take one out, or put a new one in, and watch the report change.
  </p>

  <div class="demo-frame">
    <Diagnostics />
  </div>

  <h2>What this costs</h2>

  <p>
    The module weighs about 3.7&nbsp;MB over the wire. It carries its own text
    face, its own hyphenation patterns and its own segmentation data, since
    the engine reads no files and asks its host for nothing. Nothing fetches
    it until a demo scrolls into view, and a page with no demo on it never
    does. If the browser reports a metered connection, you are asked first.
  </p>
</StarlightPage>

<style is:global>
  .demo-frame {
    margin: 1.5rem 0 2.5rem;
  }
</style>
